--
std::map with a move-only mapped type ([container.reqmts], [associative.
reqmts]: emplace needs only EmplaceConstructible; [temp.inst]/3-4: unused
members of pair<const int, move_only> and of the map are not
instantiated).  With libstdc++ 13 the verdict is wrong: `m.at(1).v' is not
5 after `m.emplace(1, move_only(5))'.  Three spurious body drops go with
it: pair's constrained default constructor (stl_pair.h:543, "found no
match for symbol 'move_only'", see
cpp11_pair_default_constructor_not_instantiated_for_vector), pair's
explicit `pair(const T1 &, const T2 &)' (stl_pair.h:568, "member
'move_only::move_only(const move_only &)' is not accessible" -- the
deleted copy constructor) and pair's operator< (stl_pair.h:835, move_only
has no operator<), the last reached through _Rb_tree's hidden friend
operator< which nothing uses (see
cpp11_hidden_friend_instantiated_only_when_used).  This is
goto_functiont in the goto-programs TUs of the round-163 audit
(cpp17_restrict_function_pointers_tu).  Not reduced: the header-free
pieces pass, and a first cvise run (/tmp/r163/red/kb_D2) degenerated --
with normal linking it removed the node insertion so that nothing is ever
stored; a reduction needs strict linking (std::map's out-of-line
_Rb_tree_insert_and_rebalance supplied by the program).
Pre-existing (round-162 snapshot bin51).
Round 163e: passes.  The three drops were removed by the pair
default-constructor fixes (__is_constructible, same-signature
constructor templates), by constructor templates no longer being copied
as ordinary constructors, and by deferring the hidden friend; together
they also fixed the verdict.  Fails on the round-163 build bin52.
